1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,840
Principal repeat play can solve any problem.

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:03,760
It's possible.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,800
It depends upon what the discount rates are.

4
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:12,760
In principle, imagine that we all live forever.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:14,560
And every human being lives forever.

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:16,360
And then we have a system where if we ever

7
00:00:16,360 --> 00:00:18,840
catch another human being doing anything uncooperative,

8
00:00:18,840 --> 00:00:21,320
then that person is ostracized by the rest of society

9
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:23,480
for eternity.

10
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:26,000
That's one where repeat can go and get us

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,080
to a cooperative outcome if we've

12
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:29,880
got all the other information and everything else.

13
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:30,880
That's what we need.

14
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:33,480
So yeah, it's possible that repeat play could solve this.

15
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:36,280
But again, remember that repeat play doesn't have to do it.

16
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:38,400
So maybe firms are just bargaining really hard

17
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,840
to get a reputation for toughness.

18
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:44,320
Maybe every, so you can actually get greater than 100%

19
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:45,440
rent dissipation.

20
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,600
Every firm could go and spend 10 times as much on getting it

21
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:53,200
as it's worth to scare off other firms and say, look,

22
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:55,640
when we set our mind on getting a privilege,

23
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:57,120
we don't back down.

24
00:00:57,120 --> 00:00:59,160
So you better back down next time.

25
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,520
So you can actually get really extreme equilibrium

26
00:01:01,520 --> 00:01:05,640
where you burn up more than exists.

27
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:07,280
Anyway, the key thing to remember

28
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:10,120
is we've actually got three different kinds of inefficiency.

29
00:01:10,120 --> 00:01:13,240
The triangle that gets so emphasized

30
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:15,640
for undergraduates especially, empirically,

31
00:01:15,640 --> 00:01:17,120
is the least important.

32
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:22,280
The other stuff, certainly the productive inefficiency

33
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:24,480
trapezoid, almost never discussed.

34
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:27,360
And again, probably just because people have some a priori idea

35
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,920
of there's no way that productively inefficient firms

36
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:31,040
can stay in business.

37
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:33,360
How about if the government doesn't allow other people

38
00:01:33,360 --> 00:01:34,240
to compete with them?

39
00:01:34,240 --> 00:01:36,520
Can they stay in business?

40
00:01:36,520 --> 00:01:40,960
How about if it is illegal to go and build a building

41
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:43,400
with more than two stories?

42
00:01:43,400 --> 00:01:46,920
Will it then be the case that firms, that builders will go

43
00:01:46,920 --> 00:01:48,880
and build buildings with 10 stories

44
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,640
because that's more productively efficient?

45
00:01:51,640 --> 00:01:52,160
Of course not.

46
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,240
You just get your building torn down by the government

47
00:01:57,240 --> 00:02:00,680
if you do it the way that is technologically better.

48
00:02:00,680 --> 00:02:02,760
What good does that do you if the government just

49
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:04,400
knocks your building down?

50
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,600
So Alex Tabarrok has this great story of in India,

51
00:02:08,600 --> 00:02:11,400
there is a building that has been built and knocked down

52
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,440
repeatedly.

53
00:02:13,440 --> 00:02:14,240
Why?

54
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,740
Well, because the people on the land cannot get the permits

55
00:02:16,740 --> 00:02:18,640
no matter what they do.

56
00:02:18,640 --> 00:02:21,800
And the rent is so high in this area

57
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,080
that you can actually recoup all the building costs

58
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:30,200
if you can just legally stall the demolition for a few years.

59
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:31,840
So basically, they have a team of lawyers

60
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:33,480
and they've got a team of builders.

61
00:02:33,480 --> 00:02:35,920
And every time it gets knocked down, basically the next day,

62
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:37,460
they've got the builders there to clear the rubble

63
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:39,400
and start building the building again.

64
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:41,720
And then in order to demolish it,

65
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:43,720
there's a three to five year process.

66
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:45,520
And while that's going on, the building

67
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:46,880
gets built and rented to people.

68
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,120
And they make back the costs of production.

69
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:52,720
And then it gets knocked over again.

70
00:02:52,720 --> 00:02:54,120
And then it resumes.

71
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:56,480
So great system, right?

72
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:57,160
OK.

73
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:58,520
All right, so this is everything that we

74
00:02:58,520 --> 00:02:59,320
need to know for the midterm.

75
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:01,360
Now we will go and take a 10 minute break.

76
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:05,880
And then we will turn to items three or two through four.

77
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:07,000
OK.

78
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,800
All right, so we finished week six.

79
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,760
And now we'll talk about the exam.

80
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:15,240
I'll tell you a bunch of bureaucratic things about it.

81
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,760
And then I'll take other questions

82
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:20,640
just about the bureaucratic parts.

83
00:03:20,640 --> 00:03:22,080
And then we'll have another section

84
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:25,680
where I take actual substantive questions about what we learned.

85
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:28,160
So the exam.

86
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:29,800
First of all, general content.

87
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:35,520
So something like 50% from lecture, 30% from homework,

88
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:38,960
about 20% for readings.

89
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:40,960
Remember how there are readings for this class?

90
00:03:40,960 --> 00:03:43,240
This is where we see whether you've in fact read

91
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:44,960
and understood them.

92
00:03:44,960 --> 00:03:46,920
Very important point.

93
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:51,680
The exam is totally open book and note.

94
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:55,640
So you are free to bring any non-electronic materials

95
00:03:55,640 --> 00:03:56,520
you want.

96
00:03:56,520 --> 00:04:00,400
However, you may not use an electronic device of any kind,

97
00:04:00,400 --> 00:04:04,080
even a calculator, because of the internet and the possibility

98
00:04:04,080 --> 00:04:07,280
that someone is actually feeding you the answers.

99
00:04:07,280 --> 00:04:10,080
So I realize that is pretty unlikely.

100
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:12,480
But nevertheless, it's just too hard.

101
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:13,880
When I first started teaching this,

102
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,480
it was just a theoretical possibility.

103
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:16,920
Now someone really could.

104
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:19,040
You could photograph the exam.

105
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,040
Someone could do it and then photograph it back.

106
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:23,040
And you could be transcribing it pretty quickly.

107
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,040
But if you were stuck on one or two questions,

108
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:27,000
it would be really easy.

109
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,200
You just move on and wait for your accomplice

110
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:33,320
to send you the correct answer.

111
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:35,040
So anyway, it's fully open book and note.

112
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:37,320
But this means that you are free to print off

113
00:04:37,320 --> 00:04:39,720
your entire hard drive if you want.

114
00:04:39,720 --> 00:04:42,240
You can bring a dictionary if you want.

115
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,600
So all this is perfectly fine.

116
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:47,000
So remember, you're only going to have 100 minutes

117
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:49,920
to do the exam.

118
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,080
So this is plenty of time for you

119
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:58,080
to double check something that you have forgotten in a panic.

120
00:04:58,080 --> 00:05:00,520
It's not enough time for you to learn the material,

121
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,520
not enough time for you to read the papers.

122
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:04,400
That's just my guess.

123
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,400
Maybe you want to write one.

124
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:07,840
That's fine.

125
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:09,000
Let's see.

126
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,840
Now, other advice.

127
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:12,640
So what's the best way to study?

128
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:16,600
So there are now something like 17 old midterms

129
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:18,720
on the class web page.

130
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:21,400
So the best thing to do would be to take those old midterms

131
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,120
under time pressure.

132
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:26,600
That's the best way to practice is by doing.

133
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:28,680
So I have thought this for a long time.

134
00:05:28,680 --> 00:05:30,680
But then I started reading Educational Psychology

135
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:32,880
and found out I was correct, namely

136
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:34,960
that merely rereading notes to yourself

137
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:37,160
does not actually teach you very much.

138
00:05:37,160 --> 00:05:39,720
You need to actually do problems, working problems.

139
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:42,120
Because if you're reading notes, it's very easy to say, yes, yes,

140
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:42,920
of course, I understand.

141
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,920
Of course, it's also obvious, of course.

142
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:46,680
And then you're given a problem asking

143
00:05:46,680 --> 00:05:47,880
you to apply it to the notes.

144
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:49,960
And that's where many people will flounder and say,

145
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:52,080
realize, wow, I don't think I know where to start.

146
00:05:52,080 --> 00:05:54,880
So working problems is the best approach.

147
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:56,800
And the time pressure is important, too.

148
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:58,880
The fact that you can work problems successfully

149
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:01,240
given unlimited time doesn't mean you can actually

150
00:06:01,240 --> 00:06:02,880
do it under test pressure.

151
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:06,360
So that's very good to do as well.

152
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:06,860
Let's see.

153
00:06:06,860 --> 00:06:08,640
More general principles.

154
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:10,520
This is one that goes beyond this class,

155
00:06:10,520 --> 00:06:12,160
but it's a great principle.

156
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:19,880
And it's called answer the question.

157
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:22,200
This might seem really obvious, especially given

158
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:23,480
how far you've gotten.

159
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:25,960
But I was just grading some homeworks today

160
00:06:25,960 --> 00:06:27,080
from another class.

161
00:06:27,080 --> 00:06:31,000
And, wow, most of them just didn't answer the question.

162
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:34,640
So for example, if there is a question that

163
00:06:34,640 --> 00:06:38,040
says give an original example of a coordination game

164
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:41,220
and discuss how you could move from a worse

165
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:43,480
equilibrium to a better one, should you

166
00:06:43,480 --> 00:06:45,600
begin by saying a coordination game

167
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:48,000
is a game with the following properties, blah, blah, blah,

168
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,280
blah, blah, blah, blah, blah?

169
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:49,800
No.

170
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:50,960
No.

171
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:52,720
Because the question did not ask you

172
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:54,480
to explain what a coordination game is.

173
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:56,320
It asked you for an example.

174
00:06:56,320 --> 00:07:00,320
So if the question says give a novel example of a coordination

175
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:02,480
game, what would be a great first sentence

176
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:04,680
for that question, for your response?

177
00:07:04,680 --> 00:07:06,760
A novel example of a coordination game is?

178
00:07:06,760 --> 00:07:07,400
Yes.

179
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:12,120
That's a wonderful, wonderful first sentence for your answer.

180
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:13,540
I know you're under test pressure.

181
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:15,120
You don't need to be eloquent.

182
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:19,440
But if you just get to the point immediately, this is great.

183
00:07:19,440 --> 00:07:23,680
Now, I try to read every word students write.

184
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:27,320
But if you go and spend half your time not answering

185
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:29,360
the question, what are the odds that your answers

186
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:31,320
are going to be competitive with those of people

187
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:32,840
who got to the point?

188
00:07:32,840 --> 00:07:33,920
It's just hard.

189
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:37,840
If you spend most of your energy giving me back story,

190
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:41,760
you're like, economics is a subject which we can.

191
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:44,400
With the sped scholastics.

192
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:48,040
And then, page after page, and then finally, oh, yeah,

193
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:49,440
what was the question again?

194
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:51,800
So don't do that.

195
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:56,760
And I'll even add in, how about answer the literal question?

196
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:59,880
The exact words, even better.

197
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:02,680
So once in a long while, someone answers a question

198
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:04,180
even more literally than I intended,

199
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:06,280
and I always give them credit.

200
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:08,720
Because I say, wow, they were even more literal

201
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:10,480
than I had expected.

202
00:08:10,480 --> 00:08:12,680
But usually, the problem is people

203
00:08:12,680 --> 00:08:14,040
don't answer a literal question.

204
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:17,040
Instead, they try to say, well, what

205
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:19,240
is the general spirit of this question?

206
00:08:19,240 --> 00:08:20,740
And then they talk about the spirit.

207
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:22,920
And again, not good.

208
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:25,640
Because if someone answers the spirit of a question

209
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:27,880
while they've not answered the question,

210
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:30,640
this leaves me wondering, did the person

211
00:08:30,640 --> 00:08:34,040
know the answer to the actual literal question?

212
00:08:34,040 --> 00:08:37,960
So I do try to figure out whether you do or not.

213
00:08:37,960 --> 00:08:43,080
But again, the easiest thing is just to remove all doubt.

214
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,960
Don't give me any room to wonder whether you

215
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:48,080
know, because you immediately did answer

216
00:08:48,080 --> 00:08:49,320
an exactly literal question.

217
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:52,880
So if it says, discuss the reason for x

218
00:08:52,880 --> 00:08:55,560
and give two examples.

219
00:08:55,560 --> 00:08:57,080
So here's the reason for this.

220
00:08:57,080 --> 00:08:58,080
Here's example one.

221
00:08:58,080 --> 00:08:59,320
Here's example two.

222
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:00,960
Literally answered.

223
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:01,520
Done.

224
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:02,760
Great.

225
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:03,240
OK.

226
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:05,320
So that's very useful.

227
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:06,760
And then there's one other thing.

228
00:09:06,760 --> 00:09:11,200
This is a intellectual failing that people generally

229
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:12,440
acquire in economics.

230
00:09:12,440 --> 00:09:15,040
Almost no one has it until they come to economics.

231
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:16,720
And then many people never lose it.

232
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:18,160
So it's bad.

233
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:20,320
So again, I was just raising hallmarks

234
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:22,640
where there's some experienced economists, more advanced

235
00:09:22,640 --> 00:09:26,720
than you, who still seem to not understand

236
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:32,520
questions that involve the real world.

237
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:35,440
So there's a question that says, give a real world

238
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:38,960
example of rent seeking.

239
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:40,400
That's kind of easy, but anyway.

240
00:09:40,400 --> 00:09:43,840
So would a good answer then be saying,

241
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:47,840
assume that there is a widget company which produces

242
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,280
and it is seeking a monopoly privilege for widget

243
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,240
production.

244
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:55,120
Is that a good example of a real world example of rent seeking?

245
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:56,880
No.

246
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:58,240
Exactly.

247
00:09:58,240 --> 00:10:02,240
So if you just make up something, or you invent numbers,

248
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:05,360
or you start saying, talk about the real world,

249
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:09,560
and then you say, well, assume zero transactions costs.

250
00:10:09,560 --> 00:10:11,360
It's very hard to give people a rich credit

251
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:14,960
for answers like this, because again, what

252
00:10:14,960 --> 00:10:18,560
could be a good real world example of rent seeking?

253
00:10:18,560 --> 00:10:22,440
Just remember, just a few minutes ago before the break.

254
00:10:22,440 --> 00:10:23,600
Comcast.

255
00:10:23,600 --> 00:10:24,280
Comcast.

256
00:10:24,280 --> 00:10:24,760
Yes.

257
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:25,400
All right, yes.

258
00:10:25,400 --> 00:10:26,880
So Comcast, trying to do that.

259
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:30,120
Or the Chinese examination system.

260
00:10:30,120 --> 00:10:33,200
Or just anything like that.

261
00:10:33,200 --> 00:10:36,880
Of course, again, in general, if you give an original example,

262
00:10:36,880 --> 00:10:38,080
that's a bit more convincing.

263
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:42,320
Usually, I will actually say, give an original example.

264
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:44,480
Because if you just spit back my own example to me,

265
00:10:44,480 --> 00:10:46,560
then I do have to wonder, does the person actually

266
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:48,000
know how to apply the concept?

267
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,520
Or are they simply using what I taught them?

268
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:53,760
Now, on the other hand, this is one kind of phrasing.

269
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:56,560
There's another kind of phrasing that says,

270
